{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793733049,"nonce":"Ad+oDSr450zqpp29QKV6dXY7nJg=","message":"Agreed by the owner 2026-11-04. Replaces the plan in comment #1 (withdrawn in #2).\n\nPrinciple: the schedule index decides only WHEN to check, never WHAT runs. A lost webhook can cost a missing Run (like a missed push) but never a Run of old config or on an old branch.\n\nSyntax: `on: cron: { schedule: \"0 2 * * *\" }` (string or list), five fields, UTC, macros @hourly/@daily/@weekly/@monthly/@yearly. `when:` still applies. The pipeline check refuses a schedule that does not parse, never fires, or can fire twice less than 5 minutes apart (the 5-minute floor, as GitHub Actions).\n\nIndex: rows per (repo, pipeline path, schedule) with next_fire_at and the head sha they were read from. Written from the push path when the push is to the repo's live default branch, and rewritten whenever a fire reads the live head (refresh on fire). Fired slots are recorded per (repo, pipeline path, slot minute) with a unique key: one Run per pipeline per minute across replicas and overlapping expressions.\n\nFire-time check, live from the forge, every time: the repo's current default branch (repo metadata, never the stored value), that branch's head, and the pipeline at that head (read only when the head moved since the rows were written). Fire only if the pipeline still has a schedule matching the slot minute. Removed/edited schedule or deleted/renamed file: the row is dropped, no Run. Old default branch gone, forge unreachable or rate limited: the slot is skipped and recorded as a refused Run. After downtime a missed slot fires once.\n\nRate limits: the check starts up to 60 s before the slot to spread top-of-hour bursts; checks go through a per-connection concurrency cap; forge GETs send If-None-Match from an ETag cache (a 304 does not count against GitHub's limit); a rate-limited connection backs off until its reset and its slots are skipped and recorded.\n\nEvent: cron carries repo, ref refs/heads/\u003cdefault\u003e, commit and schedule; allowed_refs treats it like the default branch ref. No actor. Headline = the schedule.\n\nVisibility: GET /v1/repos/{org}/{repo}/schedules lists each pipeline's schedules with the next fire and last fire; the repo page shows them, so a schedule that was never picked up is visible.\n\nNot covered on purpose: a new schedule whose webhook was lost is found only at the next push to the default branch or the next fire in that repo. No poller.","files":null}]}